program            = 004_case_ranges
area               = 08_gcc_extensions
description        = GCC case ranges (case LOW ... HIGH:) including negative, character, degenerate single-value and fallthrough ranges
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_audit_flags     = -Wall -Wextra -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror
ub_notes           <<END
UB-GATE DEVIATION AND ITS REASON. Every program in area 08_gcc_extensions drops
-pedantic from the default warning gate. Reason: the constructs under test are
non-standard GCC extensions by definition, and -pedantic exists precisely to reject
them; retaining it would fail every program in the area for doing exactly what it is
meant to do. This is the only area-wide deviation in the corpus. No other warning is
relaxed: -Wall, -Wextra, -Wconversion, -Wsign-conversion, -Wshadow and -Werror are all
retained, and this program is clean under them at -O0, -O1 and -O2. Measured with
gcc 13.3.0 and re-verified on this branch with gcc 13.4.0. The canonical record of this
deviation is the matching paragraph of impl_defined_notes below, which is the one field
this suite reads a gate reason from; it is restated here so that the whole argument for
this program's fitness as test material can be read in one place.

UB-FREEDOM ARGUMENT. Every value is a small int; the largest computation is 1 + 10, far
from INT_MIN and INT_MAX, so there is no signed overflow. No shift operator is used. No
pointer, array or aggregate is used, so there is no aliasing violation and no
one-past-end pointer. Every object is initialized before it is read. No object is
modified twice between sequence points. Every printf call receives at most one
side-effecting argument. Nothing depends on padding bytes or on the addresses of
unrelated objects. Every switch has a default label, so control flow is total for every
possible operand value, and the case ranges within any one switch never overlap, so no
label is ambiguous. The sweep loop runs from -25 to 35 inclusive and always terminates.

Every switch operand is int, never plain char. The character constants used as range
bounds have type int in C. This is deliberate: char was measured as signed on x86_64 and
i686 but unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64, so a plain-char operand could change which
range matches on different targets and manufacture a divergence caused by the test rather
than by the compiler.

The deliberate fallthrough between two case ranges carries a /* fall through */ comment,
which GCC recognizes to suppress -Wimplicit-fallthrough; -Wextra enables that warning and
-Werror makes it fatal, so the comment is required for the gate to pass.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
UB-GATE DEVIATION AND ITS REASON. Every program in area 08_gcc_extensions drops
-pedantic from the default warning gate. Reason: the constructs under test are
non-standard GCC extensions by definition, and -pedantic exists precisely to reject
them; retaining it would fail every program in the area for doing exactly what it is
meant to do. This is the only area-wide deviation in the corpus. No other warning is
relaxed: -Wall, -Wextra, -Wconversion, -Wsign-conversion, -Wshadow and -Werror are all
retained, and this program is clean under them at -O0, -O1 and -O2. Measured with
gcc 13.3.0 and re-verified on this branch with gcc 13.4.0. A gate deviation is not a
divergence: the gate is a property of the TEST MATERIAL, it is driven by the reference
compiler only, and it renders no verdict about bcc.

Nothing implementation-defined is observed, so no target restriction is needed and all
four targets are compared. Only int values are printed, so the measured width differences
cannot be reached: sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) are 4 on i686 and 8 on x86_64, aarch64
and riscv64. Plain char is never used as a switch operand or a printed value, so the
measured char-signedness difference cannot be reached. No address or pointer value is
printed.

Case range syntax requires a space on both sides of the ellipsis. Measured with
gcc 13.3.0 and re-verified with gcc 13.4.0: writing case 1...5: without spaces produces
"error: too many decimal points in number", because the lexer reads 1. followed by ..5 as
a malformed floating constant. Every range in the program keeps the spaces.

The runtime probes use volatile int operands so the optimizer cannot fold the switch away
and must emit a genuine lowering. Dense and sparse label sets select different lowering
strategies, a jump table versus a comparison chain, and only the runtime variant
exercises them.

Output is identical on all four targets at all three optimization levels.

THIS PROGRAM CARRIES NO EXPECTED-DIVERGENCE MARKER, AND THAT IS A MEASURED OUTCOME RATHER THAN AN
OVERSIGHT. A marker was carried here and has been retired, because the divergence it described was
not observed: the compiler under test accepted this program, oracle (a) agreed with the reference
compiler on all four targets at all three optimization levels, and the marker's own safeguard
therefore reported XPASS and failed the run. What follows is the whole analysis the marker carried,
kept because it is still the material a reviewer needs, together with what it would take to mint the
marker again.

WHY CASE RANGES ARE THE ONE CONSTRUCT IN THE MANDATED EXTENSION LIST THAT WAS EVER A MARKER
CANDIDATE. The mandated list of GCC extensions is statement expressions, typeof, computed gotos and
case ranges. The first three appear in the repository's documented extension inventory; case ranges
appear in none of them. docs/project-guide.md line 206 is that inventory: its Compliance and Quality
Review row for the C11 frontend records a passing verdict and enumerates exactly what is parsed --
__attribute__, statement expressions, typeof, computed goto, inline assembly -- and case ranges are
not among them. docs/technical-specifications.md lines 13, 107 and 506 enumerate the same set in
three further places, with the same absence.

WHAT THAT WOULD AND WOULD NOT ESTABLISH, STATED PLAINLY SO THE SILENCE IS NOT READ AS MORE THAN IT
IS. The candidate basis is an OMISSION, and an omission is weaker than a statement: an inventory
that does not name a feature is consistent with the feature working and the inventory being
incomplete, with the feature not working, and with nobody having considered the question. It could
never have been a claim that bcc must reject case ranges. It could only ever have been a claim that
IF bcc rejects them, the rejection is traceable to a documented gap rather than being an unexplained
divergence -- which is exactly the distinction requirement 5 draws, and exactly the ambiguity the
plan flagged for a maintainer to resolve.

WHY NO MARKER IS ATTACHED TODAY, WHICH IS THE INTERESTING PART. A marker excuses a divergence, so a
marker attached before the divergence has been seen excuses a cell on the strength of an author's
expectation. Two things then go wrong at once. If the construct in fact works, the cell agrees, the
verdict is XPASS and the run fails on a mistake in the TEST MATERIAL rather than a defect in the
compiler -- which is precisely what happened here, and it is the reason this record no longer carries
one. And for as long as such a marker goes unnoticed it blinds the suite to a genuine regression in
exactly the construct it was meant to document, because a real refusal would be excused as expected.
The correct record for an unobserved divergence is therefore no marker at all, which is the rule
tests/conformance/EXPECTED_DIVERGENCES.md section 1.1 states in as many words.

WHAT IS NOT EXCUSED, AND WHAT IS STILL EXERCISED. Constraint C3 forbids excluding a feature because
it is difficult, and nothing here is excluded: the program is written, compiled and executed on all
four targets at all three optimization levels, exactly like every other program in the corpus, and
all three oracles stay enabled -- twelve bcc cells, on the same footing as every other program. What
changed is only the CLASSIFICATION available to a divergence, and it changed in the strict direction:
a divergence of ANY class is now a FINDING. A refusal to translate this program is a FINDING; a
wrong answer from a compiler that accepts the syntax is a stdout_mismatch and was always a FINDING.
A finding is delivered with a verbatim reproducer, the captured output of each compiler and each
backend, an environment fingerprint and the exact reproduction commands, which is strictly more
evidence about the open question than an excuse would have been.

WHAT IT WOULD TAKE TO MINT THE MARKER AGAIN. Not the omission alone and not an observation alone --
both, in the order the register's add-a-marker checklist gives. Confirm the divergence is real and
reproducible from the recorded commands on a clean workspace and on more than one run, capturing the
output from each compiler and each backend. Then add the expected_divergence keys back to this
record, and the matching summary row and structured entry to
tests/conformance/EXPECTED_DIVERGENCES.md, in the shape the specification fixes for it: identifier
XD-GCCEXT-CASE-RANGES-001, class compile_failure, scope oracle_a alone -- oracle (a) is where "the
reference compiler accepted this program and the compiler under test did not" is a statement about
the two compilers, while oracles (b) and (c) merely lose their subject and are reported as dependent
blocked arms citing that root -- and a basis citing docs/project-guide.md line 206. Write the
observation as it was actually seen, in the past tense; anticipatory wording is refused at parse
time. Should the repository instead come to STATE the limitation in prose, cite that sentence and
quote it in the optional expected_divergence.documented key: the audit resolves the quotation inside
the cited range, and the basis becomes an assertion rather than a silence.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
cr_bucket_neg=10
cr_bucket_b1=10
cr_bucket_b2=10
cr_bucket_b3=1
cr_bucket_b4=10
cr_bucket_default=20
cr_hex_digit=7
cr_hex_lower=14
cr_hex_upper=11
cr_hex_none=-1
cr_single_hit=42
cr_single_miss=0
cr_fall_low=11
cr_fall_mid=10
cr_fall_high=100
cr_fall_none=0
cr_runtime_bucket=2
cr_runtime_hex=12
END
